      "content": "#### Pixel construction rules for the “lsb Unpacked” style",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "title"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 74,
      "topLeftY": 331,
      "bottomRightX": 263,
      "bottomRightY": 348,
      "content": "To construct the pixel stream:",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 84,
      "topLeftY": 349,
      "bottomRightX": 528,
      "bottomRightY": 402,
      "content": "1) Put the component in the least significant bits.\n2) Pad the most significant bits to the nearest 8-bit boundary if needed.\n3) Start with the next component on the next 8-bit boundary.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 65,
      "topLeftY": 463,
      "bottomRightX": 224,
      "bottomRightY": 482,
      "content": "#### 6.1.2 msb Unpacked",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "title"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 64,
      "topLeftY": 488,
      "bottomRightX": 736,
      "bottomRightY": 544,
      "content": "For msb unpacked, each component is filled msb first and its lsb's are zero-padded to the nearest byte (8-bit) boundary. Hence next component (or pixel) always starts on the next byte. For PFNC-compliant image buffers, msb unpacked must be explicitly specified in the pixel format name by appending \"msb\".",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 111,
      "topLeftY": 569,
      "bottomRightX": 673,
      "bottomRightY": 602,
      "content": "Note: If the component size is a multiple of 8 bits, then use lsb unpacked since no padding bits is necessary and this convention aims for the shortest string to represent the pixel name.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 65,
      "topLeftY": 638,
      "bottomRightX": 625,
      "bottomRightY": 657,
      "content": "Note that in the following figures, we put byte 0 on the left to help illustrate the concept.",
